WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.500
Disney's Oscar-winning animation Beauty And
The Beast is often considered one of the studio's

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:06.500
best.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:10.320
Still, over the years, fans have analysed
the film and uncovered numerous movie mistakes,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.430
goofs and plot holes.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:15.160
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video, I'll be explaining how

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.930
Disney's live-action remake of Beauty And
The Beast fixes many of those mistakes.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:24.360
And just before I get stuck in, be warned
there are spoilers ahead for both movies.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.860
To avoid them, you can tap or click here to
watch my spoiler-free review of Beauty And

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.390
The Beast and come back when you've seen the
films.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:35.300
Now, I invite you to relax and pull up a chair,
as I proudly present 10 movie mistakes, goofs

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.680
and plot holes fixed by the new Beauty And
The Beast movie.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:42.901
Bookworm Belle seemed to have a curious arrangement
with the bookseller in her village as she

00:00:42.901 --> 00:00:47.880
didn't appear to actually buy any books from
him, instead she borrowed books freely whenever

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:52.950
she wanted something to read, making his bookshop
more of a library than an actual business.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:57.110
The new movie cleverly fixes this by having
Belle borrow her books from the local priest

00:00:57.110 --> 00:01:01.200
Père Robert and acknowledges the original
movie mistake by having her thank him with

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.140
the words: "your library makes our small corner
of the world feel big."

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.810
In fact, it's odd that there's a bookshop
at all in the original film given how much

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:10.880
all the townspeople hate reading.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.540
And it's a call-back to this inconsistency,
when Père Robert refers to Belle as "the

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.000
only bookworm in town."

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.200
You might have enjoyed watching Gaston fall
into a muddy pond after Belle fended off his

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.890
advances in the animated movie, however, the
way it happened really doesn't make sense.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:29.570
What I mean is that he falls out of Belle's
door straight into the muddy pond, however,

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:33.740
the pond isn't anywhere near Belle's house
so that couldn't actually happen.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:37.680
This time around Gaston doesn't fall into
a pond, but he does step into a muddy puddle

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.479
just after Belle rebuffs him outside her home.

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:44.320
And notice that the filmmakers located that
muddy puddle a little way down the street

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.340
from Belle's house, very likely in recognition
of that funny incongruous moment in the original

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.670
movie.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:54.580
The time period for the original Beauty And
The Beast was never officially confirmed by

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:59.060
Disney, but the clothes, the type of gun Gaston
uses, and the use of steam-powered machinery

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:04.400
by Maurice suggests the likely time period
is the 1790s or early 1800s.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:08.670
Now, that means that the Eiffel Tower which
appears during "Be Our Guest" is an anachronism

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.739
because construction only began on it in 1887.

00:02:11.739 --> 00:02:15.989
The new movie makes a very funny correction
to this original movie mistake as it replaces

00:02:15.989 --> 00:02:20.180
the Eiffel Tower in "Be Our Guest" with a
guillotine joke when Lumiere chops a baguette

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:21.180
in two.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:25.090
The guillotine would have been historically
accurate for the animated movie because this

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:30.180
form of execution was introduced in France
during the French Revolution in 1792, which

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.950
fits into the likely time period when the
animation was set.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:38.209
Curiously though, Lumiere's use of a guillotine
joke may have introduced a new movie mistake

00:02:38.209 --> 00:02:42.510
into the live-action version, because as I
just mentioned the guillotine wasn't common

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:47.040
in France until the late 18th century, whereas
Disney has stated that the remake is actually

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.510
set in the mid-18th century.

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:53.709
By the way, chopping the baguette may also
be a little knowing joke by the filmmakers

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:55.469
about how they cut the line, 'Marie!

00:02:55.469 --> 00:02:57.790
The baguettes', from the new movie!

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:02.109
Talking of timelines, there's a significant
plot hole heavily implied in the animated

00:03:02.109 --> 00:03:03.109
movie.

00:03:03.109 --> 00:03:06.989
In that film, Lumiere revealed that the castle
has been under the Enchantress's spell for

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:08.330
ten years.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:11.959
And the narration at the beginning of the
film explained that the prince had until his

00:03:11.959 --> 00:03:15.799
21st birthday to break the spell or the curse
would become permanent.

00:03:15.799 --> 00:03:19.980
If we do the maths and subtract ten years
from 21, this suggests that the prince would

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:22.319
have been around eleven at the time of the
curse.

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:25.790
But the painting we see the Beast slash is
one of him as an adult man which doesn't make

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:27.999
sense if he was cursed as a child.

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:31.019
The new movie neatly fixes this issue in a
couple of ways.

00:03:31.019 --> 00:03:35.159
Firstly, we see that the prince is already
an adult when the Enchantress curses him,

00:03:35.159 --> 00:03:39.989
and secondly, Lumiere's line in "Be Our Guest"
changes from "Ten years we've been rusting"

00:03:39.989 --> 00:03:42.230
to "Too long, we've been rusting".

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:47.139
The ages of Chip and Mrs Potts in the animated
movie have also been questioned by fans.

00:03:47.139 --> 00:03:51.569
In that movie, Chip's voice sounds younger
than 10 years old and he also looks very young

00:03:51.569 --> 00:03:55.469
when he transforms into human form, which
implies he was born a teacup, rather than

00:03:55.469 --> 00:03:56.469
a human child.

00:03:56.469 --> 00:04:00.290
This crazy thought has spawned theories about
whether Mrs Potts the teapot could actually

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:03.159
have given birth to a teacup while enchanted!

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:07.999
Also, when she's in her human form, Mrs Potts
looks somewhat old to be Chip's mother.

00:04:07.999 --> 00:04:11.909
These age problems are fixed in the remake
as the filmmakers made a point of including

00:04:11.909 --> 00:04:16.410
a scene where Mrs Potts and Chip run in to
the ballroom as the prince is being cursed,

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:21.030
meaning that we know they both existed in
their human form/s before the curse was enacted.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:25.390
The new movie also makes a joke about the
idea that Mrs Potts was perhaps rather old

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.870
to be Chip's mother in the original movie.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:32.200
This happens when LeFou asks Mrs Potts if
she's Chip's grandmother, which infuriates

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:37.280
her so she kicks off the attack by the enchanted
objects on the villagers invading the castle.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.930
And there's another Mrs Potts goof fixed from
the animation during "Be Our Guest".

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:44.980
You'll need to look carefully, but notice
that when Mrs Potts utters her iconic line,

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:47.090
"Heaven sakes, is that a spot?"

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:49.220
this time she cleans off a dirty spot on Chip.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:50.220
Well, in the original movie, the spot she
finds and cleans is on herself.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:53.650
But watch the animation carefully just before
this moment and you'll see that even though

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:58.030
Mrs Potts hops around all over the place and
we get to see her all over, there was never

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:02.400
a spot on her at any point until she says
that line in the film.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:07.260
The new movie gives proper time on screen
to the Enchantress, with new details and clarification

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:11.470
of how the curse works helping to explain
away other plot holes in the animated version.

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:16.420
For example, in the original movie, it seemed
silly that the local town was totally unaware

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:18.200
of the nearby castle.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:22.470
This is fixed in the Enchantress's new narration
as she explains that her curse made everyone

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:27.420
in the town forget that the castle and the
servants who lived there ever existed.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.330
When Maurice can't figure out which way to
go in the original film, it seems like an

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:35.890
overwhelmingly foolish choice to take the
fork in the road that leads down a dark and

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:37.560
misty foreboding path.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:42.240
Maurice's horse Phillipe really doesn't want
to go that way either, but the animation uses

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:46.770
his master's poor choice to set up an accident
where Maurice gets separated from Phillipe

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.380
and ends up later at the Beast's castle.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:53.740
The new movie has a much better take on this
in keeping with its story, as a lightning

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:59.270
strike causes a tree to block Maurice's usual
path, which then forces him to take the alternate,

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:01.570
more ominous-looking path.

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:05.450
When Phillipe returns home to Belle in the
animation, he appears to know how to take

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.180
her to Beast's hidden castle.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:11.590
This makes no sense because Phillipe had never
been to the castle before, having bolted before

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:14.550
Maurice ever got to the Beast's enchanted
home.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:19.070
In the new film, Maurice ends up at the castle
with Phillipe, so it now makes more sense

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:21.510
that the horse would be able to direct Belle
there.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:26.270
In both movies, Beast comes to Belle's rescue
when she's attacked by wolves after trying

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:30.230
to flee the castle and, in both cases, he
gets badly injured.

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:34.610
The animation, however, skips over how Belle
managed to lift the unconscious and enormously

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:36.720
heavy Beast on to her horse.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.660
The new movie has a nice subtle remedy to
this problem when Belle goes to the injured

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:44.860
Beast and says to him, "You have to help me,
you have to stand", because it's clear that

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:49.490
there's no way Belle would be able to lift
the huge Beast by herself without his help.

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:53.550
